\section{Customization}

The default inclusive numeric search interval is \texttt{[\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}8\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}7\allowbreak{}]}. Change the global interval with \texttt{M\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}N} and \texttt{M\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}X} before including \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}, or specialize an individual enum's range:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
enum class Opcode { Load = 1000, Store = 1010 };

template <> struct dsl::customize::enum_range<Opcode> {
  static constexpr int min = 1000;
  static constexpr int max = 1010;
};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}V\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} does not scan the interval and can name enumerators outside it. Per-enum search spans must remain smaller than \texttt{U\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}6\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}X}.

Name customization supports replacement names and exclusion:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <>
constexpr dsl::customize::customize_t
dsl::customize::enum_name<Opcode>(Opcode value) noexcept {
  switch (value) {
    case Opcode::Load: return "load";
    case Opcode::Store: return dsl::customize::invalid_tag;
    default: return dsl::customize::default_tag;
  }
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{c\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} customizes a type name in the same way. \texttt{c\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h} trims a prefix from discovered names.

Ranges, flag status, and prefix trimming can also be declared through ADL in the enum's namespace:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
namespace lexer {
enum class Kind { TOK_Number = 1000, TOK_End = 1001 };

inline auto magic_enum_define_range_adl(Kind) {
  return dsl::customize::adl_info().minmax<1000, 1001>().prefix<4>();
}
}
static_assert(dsl::enum_name(lexer::Kind::TOK_Number) == "Number");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Declare all specializations and ADL hooks before the first reflection of the affected enum. \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e} is a namespace alias: use qualified specializations as above, or open \texttt{n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e}.

All upstream \texttt{M\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}*} configuration hooks are retained, including \texttt{M\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}E}, custom optional/\allowbreak{}string/\allowbreak{}string-view aliases, \texttt{M\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}H\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}H}, \texttt{M\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}H\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}H\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}W\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}H}, assertion controls, and container exception controls. Configuration must be consistent across translation units. The original per-header guards also permit including external magic\_\allowbreak{}enum v0.9.8 headers before or after \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}.

