\section{Compiler behavior and provenance}

Reflection uses upstream compiler-signature parsing (\texttt{\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}Y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}} or \texttt{\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}}). It supports GCC, Clang, and MSVC on the versions supported by magic\_\allowbreak{}enum; the surrounding MuDSL API requires C++20. Forward-declared enums cannot be reflected. Alias naming is compiler-dependent, and aliases do not add distinct values. Large scan intervals increase constexpr compilation work. See the pinned upstream \href{https://github.com/Neargye/magic\_enum/blob/v0.9.8/doc/reference.md}{reference} and \href{https://github.com/Neargye/magic\_enum/blob/v0.9.8/doc/limitations.md}{limitations} for complete overload and compiler details.

The embedded block retains all upstream MIT copyright and permission notices. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}y} recursively expands the public headers, preserves their include guards, adds optional \texttt{<\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}>} detection, and defers container-specific string requirements until those operations are used. This allows core reflection's minimal custom string aliases to coexist with the included container declarations. Its source digest pins the exact v0.9.8 headers. To reproduce or verify the block from an upstream v0.9.8 checkout:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
python3 MuDSL/scripts/update-magic-enum.py /path/to/magic_enum
python3 MuDSL/scripts/update-magic-enum.py /path/to/magic_enum --check
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The integration tests can be built independently of the other MetaTk libraries:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
cmake -S MuDSL/tests -B build/mudsl-enums
cmake --build build/mudsl-enums
ctest --test-dir build/mudsl-enums --output-on-failure
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The MetaTk build also enables these tests with \texttt{B\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}N} and the example with \texttt{B\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}X\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}N}.
